WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.110
Your class voted online

2
00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:03.210
for the school trip.

3
00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.000
The computer counted “Zoo”, “zoo” and

4
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.120
“the zoo” as three different places.

5
00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:13.750
It's the same zoo!

6
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.600
Today: messy text that breaks

7
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.200
counts, and how to clean it.

8
00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.920
Computers compare text

9
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.780
exactly, letter by letter.

10
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.660
A space at the end, a capital letter or

11
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.360
one different letter makes a new answer.

12
00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.990
People see one word; the

13
00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:34.290
computer sees three.

14
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:36.550
Clean in steps.

15
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.840
First, remove the spaces at the ends.

16
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.690
Next, make the letters match: all small in

17
00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.650
English, one form of each Arabic letter.

18
00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.630
Last, match each answer to a

19
00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.150
list of allowed answers.

20
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.640
Better still, stop it happening.

21
00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.210
Give people a list to choose

22
00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.330
from, not a box to type in.

23
00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.470
A drop-down list means nothing to clean.

24
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:04.230
Quick check!

25
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.650
To a computer, is a word with a space at

26
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.850
the end the same as the word without it?

27
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.400
No: the space is a character too,

28
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:14.940
so it's a different answer.

29
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.400
That's why cleaning starts by

30
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.460
removing the spaces at the ends.

31
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.880
Python can clean text for you.

32
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.060
strip removes the spaces at both ends.

33
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.040
lower makes every letter small.

34
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.230
Do it in a loop, and every answer

35
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.310
gets the same treatment.

36
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.060
Count again after cleaning.

37
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.890
Before: three different places.

38
00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.410
After: one place, with all nine votes.

39
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.180
Same votes, the right answer.

40
00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.130
In the app, two teachers

41
00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:49.330
typed a week's register.

42
00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.050
Tidy the names, then match

43
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:53.150
them to the class list.

44
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.360
Watch the names counted go from twenty

45
00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.580
to thirteen, and then to eight.

46
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:00.600
Your turn.

47
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.580
Which of these stops messy

48
00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.640
answers before they even start?

49
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.210
A drop-down list: nobody types,

50
00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:10.530
so nothing gets messy.

51
00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:14.450
Remember: computers compare text exactly.

52
00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.450
Clean in steps: spaces,

53
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.630
letters, then a list.

54
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.210
And in Python, strip and

55
00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:23.670
lower do the tidying.

56
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.870
Try it tomorrow: look at a sign-up

57
00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.690
sheet, or a class poll where

58
00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:31.950
people typed their answers.

59
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.000
Count how many ways one

60
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.200
answer was written.

61
00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.220
Then write the clean list.
